<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">North Rhine-Westphalia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">North Rhine-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> is the most populous state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a>. It was created by the British occupation authorities in 1946 from the northern part of the Prussian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine-province">Rhine Province</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-westphalia">Province of Westphalia</a>, and the Free State of Lippe joined it in 1947. It includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a> industrial region and the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, Dortmund and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia.jpg" alt="North Rhine-Westphalia" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bundesland Nordrhein-Westfalen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_North_Rhine-Westphalia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
